# a-wall-breaks — plan 12 B2.
#
# The first scenario in which the exchange RESOLVES.  A sealed base, one
# robot, and a wall that eventually gives: it stops at the seal, chews
# it, the wall comes down into a heap of masonry, and the robot walks
# over the heap to the core.
#
# ── What makes this one evidence ────────────────────────────────
#
# `plans/12` § Invariant gate names the failure this phase exists to
# avoid, and it is not "the wall never breaks" — it is a break that
# leaves something NOTHING CAN STAND ON.  Had a break erased its hex,
# the painted layer is sparse and sea-default, so the breach would read
# as `sea` and be strictly LESS passable than the wall it replaced,
# while "the wall broke" asserted true.  `tests/12_b0_probe.loft` § 3
# measured exactly that.
#
# So the measurement that matters here is the last one: **an enemy ends
# up INSIDE**.  `kind 2 0 grass` and `pile 2 0` say the two halves of a
# break happened; only `enemy 0 0 0` says the breach is a way in.
#
# ── Why it does not tick the whole siege out ────────────────────
#
# One robot does 1 HP/s, so even this stub is 23 ticks of arithmetic
# this run has already measured the rate of.  So it ticks far enough to
# watch the HP fall at the rate the numbers say, softens the wall with
# one `damage` line, and lets the SIEGE land the last of it.  Every
# step of the real path, none of the waiting: `damage` cannot break
# anything, only a tick can.
#
# ── The world ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   one corridor of grass, twelve hexes:  (12, 0) -> (0, 0)
#   sealed at (2, 0) with a wall          3.0 m, and a robot climbs 2.0
#   core at (0, 0), spawn at (12, 0)
#
# The corridor is one hex wide, so there is no way round: the robot's
# routing field covers only (0, 0) and (1, 0), and everything east of
# the seal has no route at all.  That is what puts it in the siege.

echo a wall breaks: the seal holds, then it does not, and the robot walks in

do toggle_mode

# The core is where the target is.
do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

# The spawn, twelve hexes out — clear of close_spawn_disable_radius
# (10) and inside the scrambler bubble (25).
# ⚠ TWICE: the place-kind cycle is spawn -> target -> tower -> spawn
# since plan 12 B5a gave towers a marker kind of their own.
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 12 0
marker 12 0 spawn 3

count markers 2 2
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag 12 0 0 0
count painted 13 13

palette 9
click 2 0
kind 2 0 wall
count painted 13 13

# ── The wave: nobody is besieging anything yet ──────────────────
#
# The reading that makes the ones after it mean something.  A wall at
# full HP twelve hexes from the nearest enemy is the state every later
# assertion is measured against.
#
# ⚠ **15 HP, not 100 — the seal is a STUB.**  `numbers.json` § wall's
# 100 is the BRACED figure, and this wall plugs a one-hex corridor with
# no structure neighbours at all: nothing to push against, 15% of the
# allowance (plan 12 B3).  A player who wants a wall to hold has to give
# it something to lean on, and that is the whole of the bracing rule
# stated as one number in a run.
wave 1
count alive 1 1
count targets 0 0
hp 2 0 15.0 15.0

# ── Nine ticks: it arrives, and has not bitten yet ──────────────
#
# ⚠ The tick lands its damage against the world it STARTED with, so the
# tick that carries the robot to (3, 0) is one in which it could still
# move — and an enemy that can move attacks nothing.  The wall is
# untouched at exactly the moment the siege begins.
tick 9
enemy 0 3 0
enemies passable
count targets 1 1
target 0 2 0
hp 2 0 15.0 15.0

# ── Three ticks of siege: 1 HP/s, and a tick is 2/3 of a second ─
#
# 3 x per_tick(1.0) = 2.0 HP.  This is the whole rate, measured — and
# it is the reason `numbers.json` goes on saying HP/s rather than
# HP/tick (`spawn.loft` § Why the SECONDS survive into the engine).
tick 3
hp 2 0 12.9 13.1
enemy 0 3 0

# ── Soften it, and let the siege finish the job ─────────────────
damage 2 0 12.5
hp 2 0 0.4 0.6

# ⚠ The tick that breaks it does NOT let the robot through.  A wall
# that fell mid-loop would open a route for whoever the roster visited
# later, and `ENEMY_MOVEMENT.md` § The tick resolves once is what says
# it must not — so the break lands after everybody has moved, and it is
# the NEXT tick that routes through the hole.
tick 1
kind 2 0 grass
pile 2 0 0.9 1.1
enemy 0 3 0
count targets 0 0

# ── And in ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Onto the heap first — a 0.99 m step, inside a robot's 2.0 m climb —
# and then down the far side to the core.  THIS is the phase: a breach
# that nothing could stand on would leave the robot at (3, 0) forever
# with every assertion above still green.
tick 1
enemy 0 2 0
enemies passable
snap b2-the-robot-on-the-breach

tick 2
enemy 0 0 0
enemies passable
count alive 1 1
range 0 0
